---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Text from '@/components/motion/scenes/Text.astro';
import { TextControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/TextControls';
import { MotionTextReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-text.tsx';
import { MotionTextAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-text.ts';
import MotionTextSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-text.svelte';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';

const defineHeadline = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// Each word rises and fades in, in turn, when the sentence enters the view.
// reduced: 'fade' fades the sentence in place under reduced motion, instead of showing it at once.
defineMotion('headline', {
  verb: 'reveal',
  split: 'words',
  reduced: 'fade',
  keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 12 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }],
});`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="text"
  title="Motion: animate text"
  description="Split a text into words, characters or lines, and play each piece in turn, while a screen reader reads the text once. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Put <code>data-motion</code> on a text element: its words, letters or lines play in turn, and a screen reader reads the text once, as written.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Call <code>useMotion</code> with the name of a split motion and put its ref on a text element: its words, letters or lines play in turn, and a screen reader reads the text once.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on a text element: its words, letters or lines play in turn, and a screen reader reads the text once, as written.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion()</code> to a text element: its words, letters or lines play in turn, and a screen reader reads the text once, as written.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="text" label="Live example: Animate text" height={360}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><TextControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Text size="page" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-text-readout="all">split: 'words' · origin: 'first' · verb: 'reveal'</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Split a text and play its pieces in turn</h2>
  <p>
    A definition with <code>split</code> cuts the text of its element into pieces, and plays its keyframes on the pieces in a
    cascade, in reading order. <code>split</code> is <code>words</code>, <code>chars</code> (the letters, as graphemes) or
    <code>lines</code>, and it applies to <code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code> and <code>reveal</code>.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={defineHeadline} data-language="ts" />
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Put <code>data-motion="headline"</code> on the text element, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page, after the definition.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Call <code>useMotion('headline')</code> in your component, and put its <code>ref</code> on the text element. Define the motion in a module that runs before the element is bound.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, and put <code>udxMotion="headline"</code> on the text element. Define the motion in a module that runs before the element is bound.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Attach the text element with <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('headline')&#125;</code>. Define the motion in a module that runs before the element is bound.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-text" previewHeight={[208, 248]}>
    <MotionTextReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionTextAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionTextSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Reveal the text as it enters the view</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>reveal</code> plays when the text enters the view. Text that is already in view when the page loads is part of the
    page, so it keeps its natural state, as the rest of the page does. To play the text as it is inserted, use
    <code>appear</code> instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Replay the text</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>: the reveal plays again from its first frame.</p>
    <p slot="react">Call <code>play()</code> on the result of <code>useMotion</code>, as the example's Replay button does, or from the <code>motionRef</code> of a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>: the reveal plays again from its first frame.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Take the directive in a template reference, <code>#headline="udxMotion"</code>, and call its <code>play()</code>, as the example's Replay button does: the reveal plays again from its first frame.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Take the element with <code>bind:this</code>, and call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2>Keep the sentence read once, as written</h2>
  <p>
    The original nodes move into a visually hidden copy, which keeps their semantics: <code>em</code>, <code>strong</code> and
    <code>abbr</code> stay as they are. The copy that the eye sees is <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>, so a screen reader reads
    the text once, as written. A change of the text is cut again.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>
    <code>split</code> is one of the options of a reveal, or of an appear. <code>stagger</code> and <code>origin</code> set the
    cascade of its pieces. With <code>lines</code>, the words that share a line play together, and a resize rebuilds the lines
    once the motion in progress has ended.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions verb="reveal" omit={['repeat', 'axis']} />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>Each piece moves and fades on the default spatial spring. The pieces cascade tight, 20 ms apart, unless the definition sets a stagger.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'stagger.tight']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Under reduced motion, the text is not split: the element plays whole. The definitions of this page use <code>reduced: 'fade'</code>, so the sentence fades in place.</li>
    <li>With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, the sentence shows at once, without a split.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the text holds a link, a button, a field or an element with a <code>tabindex</code>, the element is not split: it plays whole, and a development build says why. Put the motion on a text element that holds none of them.</li>
    <li>If you split by lines and the text holds an element other than a line break, the lines fall back to words. Remove that element, or split by words.</li>
    <ForFramework as="li">
      <Fragment slot="react">If React renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so React no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="angular">If Angular renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so Angular no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="svelte">If Svelte renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so Svelte no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
    </ForFramework>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
